Amanda, can I share screen?

00:06:37.685 --> 00:06:38.485
Here we go.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.390
>> So hopefully you can all see this. I'm going to make it a little bit larger.

00:06:52.045 --> 00:06:54.605
I'm going to hit just the highlights.

00:06:56.045 --> 00:07:01.725
You guys can do it however you want. You can hold your questions to the end or you can interrupt me as I go

00:07:02.205 --> 00:07:04.845
and I'll answer things on the fly.

00:07:05.165 --> 00:07:05.405
Whatever

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:07.830
you're comfortable with.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:10.310
All right. So

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:15.430
the bottom line is this budget represents an overall

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:18.950
one year budget increase of a little over 4%

00:07:18.615 --> 00:07:20.935
or roughly a half million dollars.

00:07:21.735 --> 00:07:23.815
Total budget as presented

00:07:24.055 --> 00:07:27.015
is 12,939,003

00:07:27.015 --> 00:07:28.135
and $92

00:07:28.135 --> 00:07:29.495
in total spending.

00:07:30.695 --> 00:07:36.970
So, just kind of back up and say, what are the trends where we've been over the last four years?

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:42.890
Over the last four budgets, which starting in twenty nineteen-twenty budget

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:44.810
through this one we're presenting,

00:07:45.305 --> 00:07:47.865
the total budget has increased from

00:07:48.105 --> 00:07:50.425
$12,370,000

00:07:50.425 --> 00:07:52.505
to $12,940,000

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:53.705
as presented.

00:07:54.505 --> 00:07:56.825
The total town taxation,

00:07:57.385 --> 00:08:00.505
which is the portion that Andover taxpayers feel directly,

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:03.330
has gone from 9,160,000

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:04.130
to

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:05.810
10,240,000

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:07.890
in that same four year period.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.810
So actual spending has increased 5%.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.610
Taxation has increased 12%.

00:08:15.145 --> 00:08:17.225
Okay. So where's the difference?

00:08:18.105 --> 00:08:21.065
That difference is largely due to

00:08:21.305 --> 00:08:23.145
the amount of money we're

00:08:23.145 --> 00:08:24.905
adding to the budget

00:08:25.865 --> 00:08:27.305
from the unexpended

00:08:27.305 --> 00:08:28.425
fund balance

00:08:28.585 --> 00:08:30.185
to offset taxation.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.040
As of four years ago, we still had quite a bit of surplus,

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.960
and we burned that surplus down over four years to keep

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.720
the taxation

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.040
lower, not the overall budget lower, but the amount of actual taxation.

00:08:46.345 --> 00:08:49.225
So in that same timeframe,

00:08:49.865 --> 00:08:53.305
inflation has ranged from low 1.4%

00:08:53.305 --> 00:08:55.065
to a high of 7%

00:08:55.065 --> 00:08:58.585
over those four years. It's been a little over 13%

00:08:58.585 --> 00:08:59.385
total.

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:00.745
So if you look,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.060
the actual budget increase

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:07.260
has been half of what inflation has been.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.020
However,

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:10.620
the taxation,

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:11.500
what

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:15.135
taxpayers actually feel has been essentially

00:09:15.775 --> 00:09:17.855
the same as inflation.

00:09:18.895 --> 00:09:23.215
So, if you take the overall budget and you say, what are the biggest

00:09:25.935 --> 00:09:27.215
parts of the budget?

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.940
Number one, the town budget is proposed to increase

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:34.020
about 10.5%,

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:39.460
which equates to about $363,000

00:09:40.795 --> 00:09:45.115
to a total of $3,770,000

00:09:45.675 --> 00:09:49.195
The Andover Elementary School budget increases

00:09:49.595 --> 00:09:51.515
5.65%

00:09:52.475 --> 00:09:53.275
or

00:09:53.355 --> 00:09:55.275
$220,000

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:59.150
to $4,120,000

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.510
That's presuming that the budget that

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:06.750
is being presented tonight is the one that passes,

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.430
the AES school board.

00:10:09.385 --> 00:10:11.785
And the RAM budget decreases

00:10:11.785 --> 00:10:13.385
1.4%

00:10:13.465 --> 00:10:18.745
or $71,800

00:10:19.225 --> 00:10:23.770
And that total budget is $5,050,000

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:27.210
The reason the RAM budget is decreasing,

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:30.570
it's not their operating budget that's decreasing,

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:33.130
it's the fact that they are coming off of

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:38.010
We are a long way through paying off the bond for RAM.

00:10:38.695 --> 00:10:41.415
So they're

00:10:41.415 --> 00:10:44.695
actually going up about $160,000

00:10:44.695 --> 00:10:47.255
something like that in operating.

00:10:47.895 --> 00:10:48.455
But

00:10:48.855 --> 00:10:50.135
because we're taking

00:10:50.375 --> 00:10:52.375
a pretty significant bond

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:53.910
payment away,

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:55.590
the net is

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:57.110
a 1.4%

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:57.990
decrease.

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:01.350
Any questions on that?

00:11:05.845 --> 00:11:13.205
So town spending, as usual, we break it down into a capital budget and an operational budget.

00:11:13.685 --> 00:11:17.845
The capital spending increases 22%

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.320
or $195,000

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.200
There's kind of two main cost drivers. The first is that

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.040
the past four years,

00:11:29.775 --> 00:11:33.775
the recommended capital budget has ended up getting cut

00:11:34.175 --> 00:11:37.855
by both the Board of Selectmen and Board of Finance every year.

00:11:38.655 --> 00:11:42.335
So what we've actually asked for hasn't increased very much.

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:47.380
It's just that it hasn't actually been funded up to this point.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.900
This year, there are two things that are increasing.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.420
The first is the public works equipment fund

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.620
and the second is in the roadwork fund.

00:11:58.445 --> 00:12:00.685
So, the public works equipment fund,

00:12:01.885 --> 00:12:03.965
that's due to the fact that

00:12:04.285 --> 00:12:04.845
the

00:12:05.565 --> 00:12:12.765
bids in the numbers we're getting back right now for purchasing capital equipment have gone up really dramatically.

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:18.160
It's most obvious in comparing plow trucks.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.240
The last plow truck we got in 2019,

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:29.360
we paid around $193,000

00:12:28.845 --> 00:12:30.605
That plow truck today

00:12:30.845 --> 00:12:33.645
is two sixty five out the door

00:12:34.285 --> 00:12:36.925
built identically to that first one.

00:12:37.245 --> 00:12:39.965
So, you know, we're seeing things

00:12:40.765 --> 00:12:42.765
when you can even get them

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:45.730
considerably more expensive.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:52.530
And we're getting that both for spare parts and for acquisitions.

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:57.970
What's really interesting to me is that when I'm getting quotes back from contractors,

00:12:58.495 --> 00:13:01.855
it's none of these good for thirty days or ninety days.

00:13:02.815 --> 00:13:08.255
I've gotten ones where the contractor is willing to guarantee prices for five days.

00:13:09.695 --> 00:13:12.490
And that's across the board from fertilizer

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:13.210
through

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:17.610
building supplies.

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:21.130
The second thing is that

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.130
we're recognizing

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:24.570
that while

00:13:25.425 --> 00:13:29.345
we've stemmed the bleeding with roadwork, in other words,

00:13:29.585 --> 00:13:31.265
we're

00:13:31.265 --> 00:13:35.585
making a positive impact in the overall road network quality,

00:13:35.985 --> 00:13:38.465
we're not making progress

00:13:38.465 --> 00:13:42.700
very fast and we've had to make a lot of compromises to do it.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:47.660
We're also faced with an increase in price of asphalt,

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:59.295
and that was before the giant spike in fuel. So I suspect that's going to make the situation either even worse because one of our biggest single expenses

00:13:59.375 --> 00:14:02.175
is actually the cost of liquid asphalt.

00:14:02.415 --> 00:14:05.535
And that tracks to some extent with petroleum.

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:06.870
So

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:09.670
depending on what happens right now,

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:14.310
it may even be a lot worse than what we're forecasting.

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:15.830
But

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:22.415
it's also six months to a year and a half away, so it's kind of hard to tell where that's going.

00:14:24.335 --> 00:14:24.975
Those

00:14:25.455 --> 00:14:28.495
are the big increases in capital spending.

00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:32.175
So in the operational side of things,

00:14:32.415 --> 00:14:37.135
we're projecting about $170,000

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.720
increase in total spending.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:45.520
However, it's actually kind of more than that, but it's moderated by two things.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:47.840
One, we are retiring the

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:49.840
AES school bond.

00:14:51.075 --> 00:14:52.995
And the second thing is we

00:14:53.155 --> 00:14:58.595
are just about to make our last payment in this fiscal year for the public works

00:14:58.755 --> 00:14:59.555
loader.

00:14:59.715 --> 00:15:01.315
So, those things together

00:15:01.955 --> 00:15:04.675
are about $120,000

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:11.510
So, without those two items coming off the books this year, spending would be up almost $300,000

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:13.750
in the operational portion of the budget.

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:16.390
So, why?

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:20.390
So, the first thing is the major expenses,

00:15:21.725 --> 00:15:24.685
the major increase is in public works.

00:15:25.725 --> 00:15:27.405
The board of selectmen

00:15:28.045 --> 00:15:31.965
would very much like to hire another full time public works member

00:15:32.365 --> 00:15:34.845
that would be hired on as a maintainer one.

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:37.490
That cost

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:40.370
is somewhere between 70,000 and $80,000

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:48.050
depending on whether the guy is single, married, or married with children based on the contributions to healthcare costs.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:54.535
And the second thing they would like to do is a full time summer helper for public works

00:15:55.255 --> 00:16:01.095
to handle some of the grunt work and get that away from the higher paid union positions.

00:16:01.895 --> 00:16:06.855
There's also fairly large increases for both vehicle and building maintenance costs,

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:11.270
just because we're having trouble keeping up with both of those right now.

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:15.110
Again, because our costs

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:18.550
are going up pretty dramatically for parts and supplies.

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:25.575
So all total, the public works operating budget increases about $145,000

00:16:25.815 --> 00:16:26.935
So that's

00:16:27.255 --> 00:16:28.295
a pretty huge

00:16:29.015 --> 00:16:29.895
increase.

00:16:30.535 --> 00:16:32.455
We can talk about specifics

00:16:33.015 --> 00:16:34.855
later and justification

00:16:35.095 --> 00:16:36.135
if you guys would like.

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:41.480
The other changes are mainly related to staffing levels.

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:43.320
As you know,

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.280
for the last two and a half years,

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:51.160
the Board of Selectmen has been in the mode of trying to reduce headcount

00:16:51.715 --> 00:16:55.315
and transition employees from full time to part time.

00:16:56.355 --> 00:16:57.235
Unfortunately,

00:16:57.235 --> 00:16:59.555
they've really started to feel the backlash

00:16:59.555 --> 00:17:07.930
for doing that from the public because frankly, we just haven't been able to provide the level of service that people in town actually want.

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:10.570
So the Board of Selectmen

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:13.050
this year asked me,

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:16.730
when I presented my budget, to also present

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:20.970
what I would consider the biggest priorities are for staffing.

00:17:21.835 --> 00:17:25.435
I provided them with that and then they made decisions

00:17:25.755 --> 00:17:29.435
about what they would and wouldn't consider for staffing for this year.

00:17:32.555 --> 00:17:39.670
Last year we had attempted to combine the position of the assistant town clerk and the building department admin.

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:41.510
Frankly, that was

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:42.950
disaster,

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:49.030
and we ended up changing course this fall and re splitting those positions,

00:17:49.915 --> 00:17:52.075
which has gone a long way towards

00:17:52.395 --> 00:17:54.475
calming things down in the town hall.

00:17:56.555 --> 00:17:57.835
So this

00:17:58.875 --> 00:18:05.275
also represents a series of changes that some that we've made already and some that we would

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:08.000
anticipate making in the next budget.

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:12.480
The first thing is our animal control officer quit.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:20.320
Actually, what he did is he came back to the town and he said he wanted his pay to be, oh, two or three times higher than it was.

00:18:23.095 --> 00:18:26.215
So we looked around and in

00:18:26.295 --> 00:18:27.815
our case, given the

00:18:28.215 --> 00:18:32.535
cost increase we would have had to retain the existing personnel,

00:18:33.095 --> 00:18:34.535
we elected

00:18:34.535 --> 00:18:34.775
to

00:18:36.310 --> 00:18:37.030
use

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:39.110
NECOG,

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:41.270
which is one of our neighboring

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.990
Capital Regional Council of Governments,

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:48.870
their shared service for the animal control officer in Kennel.

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:50.870
Good and bad to that.

00:18:52.175 --> 00:18:56.495
The good part of that is that they have professionally run kennels

00:18:56.495 --> 00:18:59.615
and it's a fully staffed 20 fourseven operation

00:18:59.855 --> 00:19:02.335
and it's seamless from the town's perspective.

00:19:03.775 --> 00:19:05.375
The downside is

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:09.460
the closest kennel is a fairways away from Andover.

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:11.460
So if a dog is

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:13.540
or an animal is

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:16.740
captured and kenalized,

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:20.580
people have to go a ways to get their pets back. So that's kind of a downer.

00:19:21.395 --> 00:19:23.955
The second thing we did is our mid

00:19:24.675 --> 00:19:27.395
year this year, our cleaning service quit

00:19:27.875 --> 00:19:32.115
basically because they got a little bit of a kerfuffle with the town clerk.

00:19:33.315 --> 00:19:35.635
And as a result, we went out to bid

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:36.850
for

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:39.730
both a cleaning service,

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:43.010
and then I also looked around and said, Could we

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:45.090
do a custodian

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:45.970
instead?

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:48.930
And we ended up switching from

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:51.410
a company, a shared service,

00:19:51.945 --> 00:19:54.745
to actually hiring a part time custodian.

00:19:56.185 --> 00:19:58.345
The costs for the cleaning portion

00:19:58.825 --> 00:20:01.305
are roughly the same as they were.

00:20:01.545 --> 00:20:06.345
But what we've also gotten is somebody that can do an awful lot of the light maintenance tasks

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:12.890
that we formerly were having to use public works or go out to vendors to do.

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:14.650
So that

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:16.730
has worked out really well,

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:20.090
both keeping the library and the town hall clean.

00:20:20.705 --> 00:20:25.345
So we would like to keep that going. I think everybody's

00:20:25.345 --> 00:20:27.905
extremely happy with the individual we have.

00:20:28.545 --> 00:20:33.265
And we've been able to get a lot of the projects in the backlogs of things like

00:20:33.425 --> 00:20:34.785
painting at town hall,

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:36.260
just

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:38.100
a lot of the little things.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:43.780
We've had some drywall repair that needed in the restrooms and things of that nature.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:45.620
So that's worked out pretty well.

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:49.220
The second thing is we kind of recognize

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:49.780
that

00:20:50.175 --> 00:20:51.535
the

00:20:52.575 --> 00:20:58.095
person that was working as the CEO, the zoning enforcement officer in the town,

00:20:58.655 --> 00:21:01.375
was working way more than ten hours a week

00:21:02.015 --> 00:21:06.330
for the town. Now, he was kind of willing to do that because he was a town

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:07.370
resident,

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:08.410
but

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:10.810
we also realized we

00:21:12.010 --> 00:21:13.370
have a lot more need

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:15.050
for that position

00:21:15.605 --> 00:21:17.125
than we had funding.

00:21:17.845 --> 00:21:21.045
So the Board of Selectmen asked to increase

00:21:21.365 --> 00:21:24.805
the hours for that position from ten to fifteen a week,

00:21:25.845 --> 00:21:26.965
which we've done so.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:31.020
Item

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:32.380
And number four

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.220
is we use

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:36.140
a building official

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:39.180
as a shared service from the town of Bolton.

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:43.765
They actually cover four towns as the building official, official,

00:21:44.325 --> 00:21:48.245
and then they do supplemental help to a couple other towns.

00:21:51.125 --> 00:21:53.285
When we first took it on, because

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:59.140
we had a fairly limited budget for that in our previous

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:03.860
building official only worked about five hours a week.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.780
We kind of looked at the size of Andover

00:22:08.025 --> 00:22:11.945
and the number of permits pulled compared to the other towns they covered.

00:22:13.065 --> 00:22:18.585
Bolton initially came back and said, Well, this is probably about seven hours of work a week.

00:22:20.345 --> 00:22:36.570
I just said flat out at this point we have funding for five. So we tried it out at five and we've done that for about a year and a half. But the reality is we're taking up a lot more of their time than we're paying for them. So if we're going to keep them on as a building official,

00:22:37.565 --> 00:22:40.685
we're going to end up increasing that to five to seven,

00:22:41.085 --> 00:22:46.685
but that will also allow us to give some regular hours in the building office

00:22:46.765 --> 00:22:49.725
from the building officials. It'll

00:22:49.725 --> 00:22:51.965
be a good thing on our end also.

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:57.600
And number five, one of the biggest complaints I've gotten from employees

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:02.960
the last year and a half has been just a lack of IT support in general.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:05.040
And I

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:08.155
can tell you that the last time I got a new computer,

00:23:08.955 --> 00:23:10.475
I waited six weeks

00:23:10.715 --> 00:23:12.555
before I had enough time

00:23:12.875 --> 00:23:17.675
from David, our IT guy, to actually install it and switch computers.

00:23:18.635 --> 00:23:23.760
And so what's happening is employees are waiting a long time to get their problems addressed.

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:27.040
And it's not that South Windsor is doing a bad job.

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:37.600
It's simply that we've only contracted for eight hours a month of total IT time. So basically, we have one person in the building four hours every two weeks.

00:23:38.225 --> 00:23:41.825
And so, we're asking to increase that by about 50%.

00:23:42.945 --> 00:23:43.585
So,

00:23:44.785 --> 00:23:47.665
in terms of personnel changes, those are the major

00:23:47.905 --> 00:23:49.025
personnel changes.

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:56.960
Like everybody, we're looking at really significantly higher costs for heating fuel, diesel, and electricity.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.080
Heating fuel and diesel fuel,

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:03.440
we're very fortunate in that

00:24:04.715 --> 00:24:09.275
we have an arrangement where we can lock in our costs considerably ahead of time.

00:24:10.075 --> 00:24:15.435
And we have locked in for next year already. We did that almost two months ago.

00:24:17.915 --> 00:24:22.930
Obviously compared to what costs are today, we're locked in at way lower

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:26.850
than what our

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:30.850
current costs would be if we were just on the market.

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:36.745
But if you compare last year's lock in to this year's lock in, it's up almost 25%.

00:24:37.065 --> 00:24:38.585
So it's pretty significant.

00:24:40.345 --> 00:24:43.465
I think, yeah, right about 25%.

00:24:44.265 --> 00:24:45.705
And we know electrical

00:24:46.505 --> 00:24:48.745
costs have gone up pretty considerably.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:52.610
So we're just flat out dealing with inflation

00:24:52.930 --> 00:24:56.690
and facing higher costs in a lot of the commodities

00:24:56.690 --> 00:24:57.970
that we utilize.

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:02.450
Okay. So on the revenue side,

00:25:03.205 --> 00:25:08.805
we did something we hadn't done in a couple of years, which was spend quite a bit of time

00:25:09.685 --> 00:25:12.885
going through all the revenue streams that we have

00:25:13.445 --> 00:25:15.205
and kind of updating the numbers.

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:22.700
So the flip side of that is, and part of the reason we did that is we've noticed the last couple of years,

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:24.620
we have underestimated

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:26.460
how much revenue we're

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:27.340
going to take in.

00:25:28.955 --> 00:25:29.675
So

00:25:30.635 --> 00:25:36.475
the total revenue that we're expected to take in has gone up about $155,000

00:25:37.675 --> 00:25:44.810
and that is due to a couple of things. One, we're taking in more to the building department because we've restructured the fees there.

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:48.570
And the second thing is we took in a lot more money

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:51.530
in the town clerk's office

00:25:51.770 --> 00:25:53.610
because there's been a lot more

00:25:55.155 --> 00:25:56.435
home selling

00:25:56.755 --> 00:26:00.995
and we do get a kickback basically every time a house gets sold

00:26:02.355 --> 00:26:03.395
in town.

00:26:03.875 --> 00:26:04.435
So

00:26:05.475 --> 00:26:09.075
those two things are hard to predict. They're kind of volatile.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.030
So, what we did is we basically looked at last year's

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:15.870
revenues,

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:21.390
projected revenues from both those things, or basically the last twelve months,

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:23.710
and then projected that forward.

00:26:25.195 --> 00:26:28.635
The next thing on the revenue side is

00:26:29.035 --> 00:26:29.675
the

00:26:30.955 --> 00:26:36.155
kind of additional funding we get that we don't normally account for in the budget.

00:26:36.555 --> 00:26:38.635
The first is the ARP funds.

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:44.190
The town last year received just about a half $1,000,000

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:48.510
from the federal ARP fund, we're going to get another half million this

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:49.550
spring.

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:58.455
The Board of Selectmen voted at their last meeting essentially to allocate 100% of that towards the cost of building a community center

00:26:59.175 --> 00:27:04.535
with the intention of building this community center without affecting taxation

00:27:04.775 --> 00:27:06.855
in this budget or the next budget.

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:08.950
So,

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:11.670
other funding sources we have available,

00:27:12.550 --> 00:27:13.510
LOTSIP.

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.030
For those of you who don't know,

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:21.350
that is one of the state standard funding mechanisms for the town.

00:27:22.005 --> 00:27:22.725
They

00:27:23.205 --> 00:27:24.485
add money

00:27:24.565 --> 00:27:31.525
to a fund every year for the town. And then when the town has larger projects they want to tap,

00:27:31.845 --> 00:27:32.325
they

00:27:33.525 --> 00:27:33.925
can

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:37.040
use that money to pay for it.

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:42.640
Currently, the balance in the LOCIP fund is about $141,000

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:45.200
so it's fairly significant at this point.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:50.405
The last time we tapped it was two years ago. We tapped it for

00:27:51.125 --> 00:27:54.485
the work that was done to reconstruct

00:27:54.805 --> 00:27:57.125
some of the transfer station.

00:27:58.005 --> 00:27:59.365
>> Isn't

00:27:59.365 --> 00:28:03.045
that supposed to be targeted mostly towards capital for transportation

00:28:03.045 --> 00:28:05.250
stuff? Isn't that by the definition?

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:08.290
A little more fluid than that flexible.

00:28:09.010 --> 00:28:09.810
>> No.

00:28:12.210 --> 00:28:16.690
You know what? That's a misprint on my part. It's LOSIP, not LOTSIP.

00:28:17.215 --> 00:28:18.575
LOTSIP is

00:28:19.375 --> 00:28:20.895
a transportation

00:28:21.375 --> 00:28:22.335
fund.

00:28:22.495 --> 00:28:24.015
LOTSIP is

00:28:27.055 --> 00:28:28.655
a more flexible fund.

00:28:29.135 --> 00:28:30.255
So that's my error.

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:33.040
I just put a D there when I shouldn't have.

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:36.880
The last thing is that we still have a steep grant

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:44.665
that we should be spending this year to help pay for road construction of part of Long Hill and

00:28:44.665 --> 00:28:46.185
then part of

00:28:47.465 --> 00:28:49.145
Shawty Mill Road.

00:28:51.385 --> 00:28:56.025
So those are kind of the major funding sources. We did have another grant in

00:28:56.370 --> 00:28:58.130
with the DEEP

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:02.930
to fund a trail design study along Route 316.

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:06.770
Just got word last week that did not get funded.

00:29:07.010 --> 00:29:08.610
So that's kind of unfortunate.

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:11.490
We did also this year get

00:29:13.125 --> 00:29:14.485
a transportation

00:29:14.485 --> 00:29:15.205
grant

00:29:15.445 --> 00:29:18.245
that will purchase for the town

00:29:18.645 --> 00:29:19.285
a

00:29:19.445 --> 00:29:20.965
new senior

00:29:21.525 --> 00:29:23.125
handicapped accessible

00:29:23.285 --> 00:29:25.045
12 passenger bus.

00:29:25.450 --> 00:29:28.570
So that's good. That was about a $75,000

00:29:28.570 --> 00:29:30.330
grant that we got for that.

00:29:31.210 --> 00:29:32.090
And that's

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:38.170
because of the additional funding from the federal government, that's 100% grant

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:43.155
this time around so the town doesn't have to match 20% like it has in the past.

00:29:43.555 --> 00:29:44.995
So that's been good.

00:29:47.235 --> 00:29:48.195
Okay.

00:29:49.635 --> 00:29:50.355
So

00:29:50.755 --> 00:29:56.430
let's talk about the mill rate a little bit and how this propagates down to the mill rate.

00:29:57.070 --> 00:30:00.670
So as we all know, the grand list is up significantly

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:03.390
because this was a revaluation

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:04.110
year.

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:06.590
And as most of you are aware,

00:30:07.495 --> 00:30:09.895
when you look at the Case Shiller Index

00:30:10.135 --> 00:30:11.335
in Connecticut,

00:30:11.335 --> 00:30:12.055
the average

00:30:12.615 --> 00:30:15.815
home price has gone up somewhere around 30%.

00:30:16.295 --> 00:30:19.015
Overall, Andover is a little bit lower than that

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:20.570
on average.

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:23.690
But obviously,

00:30:23.690 --> 00:30:24.730
some properties,

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:27.210
especially in the Lake area,

00:30:28.010 --> 00:30:29.290
gone up in

00:30:29.370 --> 00:30:30.330
costs

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:31.690
significantly.

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:33.450
So,

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:38.715
the total grand list went from $271,000,100

00:30:39.595 --> 00:30:42.555
last year to $310,000,000.

00:30:43.755 --> 00:30:46.155
So, given that $310,000,000

00:30:46.155 --> 00:30:47.115
and

00:30:47.355 --> 00:30:50.155
the increase in the budgeted amount,

00:30:51.490 --> 00:30:53.090
The mill rate

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:56.130
for the budget that's presented here

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:57.810
would be a

00:30:57.970 --> 00:30:59.330
33

00:30:59.330 --> 00:30:59.970
rate,

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:05.490
which is a decrease of 8% from last year because last year it was 36.

00:31:05.655 --> 00:31:11.975
Percent. However, the other thing to remember is that the final budget last year that we approved,

00:31:12.375 --> 00:31:15.255
because the Board of Selectmen had to approve it

00:31:15.575 --> 00:31:19.735
to get the tax bills out on time before the public actually approved the budget,

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:21.560
was

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:28.040
higher than the actual one approved and was higher than taxation.

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:34.040
So customers will get what on last year would have been a 0.13 mil lower

00:31:34.475 --> 00:31:35.915
rate this year.

00:31:36.315 --> 00:31:36.955
So,

00:31:37.435 --> 00:31:42.075
actual mil rate this year with this budget would be 32.89

00:31:42.075 --> 00:31:42.635
because

00:31:43.035 --> 00:31:46.315
the taxpayers would be getting the 0.11

00:31:46.795 --> 00:31:47.595
rebate

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:48.940
essentially.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:51.580
Anybody have any questions about that?

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:55.340
Okay.

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:01.100
>> So if we look at where the big changes

00:32:01.725 --> 00:32:06.205
come, a lot of it comes from the capital fund allocation.

00:32:06.925 --> 00:32:09.245
And so, what I did is I put

00:32:10.045 --> 00:32:10.925
basically

00:32:11.565 --> 00:32:12.525
the last

00:32:13.325 --> 00:32:15.405
three years' worth of

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:18.790
what was proposed,

00:32:19.430 --> 00:32:20.710
what was funded,

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:23.750
and then what we're proposing now.

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:29.270
And then the last column is the current balance, although that's a month or two out of date at this point.

00:32:31.175 --> 00:32:35.175
So, I'll just go through it. Basically, what we're doing is we're

00:32:35.175 --> 00:32:39.335
recommending that we spend $115,000

00:32:39.415 --> 00:32:42.055
on the Public Works Capital Equipment Fund,

00:32:42.535 --> 00:32:43.655
dollars 100,000

00:32:43.655 --> 00:32:45.095
on the Fire Engine Fund,

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:47.280
dollars 60,000

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:48.480
townwide

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:49.1000
for building maintenance,

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:52.640
and $120,000

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:54.880
for the Bunker Hill Bridge replacement.

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:58.480
This actually may end up being too low,

00:32:59.325 --> 00:33:01.725
but it's looking at this point like

00:33:02.205 --> 00:33:06.365
the design is taking long enough. The bridge actual replacement

00:33:06.445 --> 00:33:08.605
will get put off one more year.

00:33:08.925 --> 00:33:13.340
So, we actually have two more budget years to pay for the Bunker Hill Bridge.

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:16.140
For those of you who didn't go

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:18.060
to that presentation,

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:27.020
the latest cost estimate puts that about $3,000,000

00:33:27.315 --> 00:33:29.715
just for construction of that bridge.

00:33:30.995 --> 00:33:32.115
Dollars 100,000

00:33:32.115 --> 00:33:34.115
for the bridge and culvert fund.

00:33:34.995 --> 00:33:36.355
Dollars 75,000

00:33:36.355 --> 00:33:37.555
for tree work

00:33:38.355 --> 00:33:42.835
increased the roadwork fund to $330,000

00:33:43.290 --> 00:33:44.650
Town Aid Road,

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:47.210
Reval, and POCD implementation

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:47.930
fund

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:49.770
stayed the same.

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:52.010
Reval,

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:54.490
you can see we haven't quite finished.

00:33:55.145 --> 00:34:01.385
I think we have one more bill remaining for the reval, but we're probably going to have 7,000 or $8,000

00:34:01.385 --> 00:34:02.345
left over.

00:34:03.545 --> 00:34:04.985
We have a start

00:34:05.545 --> 00:34:08.665
for funding for the next reval, which will be in five years.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:10.860
And POCD

